---
/**
 * The founder's working-style report, as a printable document.
 *
 * A route plus `@page` CSS plus the browser's own "Save as PDF", rather than a
 * PDF built server-side. That decision is worth writing down:
 *
 *   Nothing is stored. A server-rendered PDF is a second copy of a private
 *   result living outside the guarantee that
 *   test/working-genius-privacy.test.ts holds, and the moment it exists
 *   somebody has to decide where it lives and for how long. This is generated
 *   on demand, handed over, and kept nowhere.
 *
 *   No new runtime. The Dockerfile is slim and a headless browser would be the
 *   single largest thing in it, for output that would be worse: the browser's
 *   own export gives selectable text, and a rasteriser gives a picture of text.
 *
 *   It reads from the session, not from a query parameter naming a founder.
 *   There is no id in the URL to change.
 *
 * The whole document is light. The app is dark-themed and printing dark-on-dark
 * gives either an unreadable page or a cartridge emptied on one report.
 */
import { getSessionUser } from "../lib/auth";
import { getWorkingGenius, listWorkingGeniusTakes } from "../db/index";
import { WORKING_GENIUS_ITEMS, WIDGET_ORDER, typeById, bandCopy, type WorkingGeniusResult, type WorkingGeniusId } from "../lib/workingGenius";
import { PAIRINGS, pairingFor, pairingTypes } from "../lib/workingGeniusPairs";

const session = await getSessionUser(Astro.cookies, Astro.request);
if (!session) return Astro.redirect("/");

/*
 * Which take to print. `take` names a date from the founder's own history, so
 * a past result can be printed from WgHistory; anything that is not one of
 * their own dates falls back to the current one rather than erroring, because
 * an edited URL is not worth a stack trace.
 *
 * Note what is absent: any parameter naming a *founder*. The row comes from
 * the session and nothing else, so there is nothing in this URL to tamper with.
 */
const wanted = Astro.url.searchParams.get("take");
const current = getWorkingGenius(session.email);
const takes = listWorkingGeniusTakes(session.email);

let raw = current?.result_json ?? null;
if (wanted) {
  const match = takes.find((t) => t.taken_on === wanted);
  if (match?.result_json) raw = match.result_json;
}

let result: WorkingGeniusResult | null = null;
try {
  result = raw ? (JSON.parse(raw) as WorkingGeniusResult) : null;
} catch {
  result = null;
}

/* Their name, never their email. An email address in a document somebody
   prints and leaves on a desk is a different thing from one on a screen. */
const displayName = session.name?.trim() || "";

const ranked = result ? result.ranking.map((id) => typeById(id)) : [];
const pairing = result ? pairingFor(result.ranking[0]!, result.ranking[1]!) : null;
const others = result ? PAIRINGS.filter((p) => p !== pairing) : [];

const pct = (n: number) => `${Math.round(n * 100)}%`;
const bandName = (b: string) =>
  b === "genius" ? "Where your energy comes from"
  : b === "competency" ? "What you can do without cost"
  : "What drains you";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="referrer" content="no-referrer" />
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow" />
    <title>Where your energy goes</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/fonts/fonts.css" />
    <style>
      /* A light document, printed on paper, not the dark app. */
      @page { size: A4; margin: 18mm 16mm; }

      :root {
        --ink: #14161a;
        --ink-2: #3c4148;
        --ink-3: #6b7178;
        --rule: #d7dade;
        --accent: #4a55b8;
        /* One face, here too. A serif would read well on an A4 sheet, but
           this report is the printed form of a screen the founder has just
           been reading, and two typographic identities for one document is
           the thing the rebuild was undoing. */
        --serif: Inter, ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
        --sans: Inter, ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
      }

      * { box-sizing: border-box; }
      html, body { margin: 0; background: #fff; }
      body {
        font-family: var(--sans);
        color: var(--ink);
        font-size: 11.5pt;
        line-height: 1.55;
        -webkit-print-color-adjust: exact;
        print-color-adjust: exact;
      }

      .sheet { max-width: 178mm; margin: 0 auto; padding: 16mm 8mm 24mm; }

      /* One section per page. break-after is the modern spelling; page-break
         stays for anything that only knows the old one. */
      section { page-break-after: always; break-after: page; }
      section:last-of-type { page-break-after: auto; break-after: auto; }

      /* A heading must never be the last thing on a page. */
      h1, h2, h3 { page-break-after: avoid; break-after: avoid-page; }
      h2, h3, p, li, tr, .card { page-break-inside: avoid; break-inside: avoid; }

      h1 { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 30pt; font-weight: 900; line-height: 1.05; letter-spacing: -0.02em; margin: 0 0 6pt; }
      h2 { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 17pt; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: -0.01em; margin: 0 0 10pt; }
      h3 { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.04em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-3); margin: 18pt 0 6pt; }
      p { margin: 0 0 9pt; }
      .lede { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 13pt; color: var(--ink-2); }
      .kicker { font-size: 8.5pt; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.18em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-3); margin: 0 0 8pt; }
      .rule { height: 1px; background: var(--rule); margin: 14pt 0; border: 0; }
      .quiet { color: var(--ink-3); font-size: 10pt; }

      /* Cover */
      .cover { display: flex; flex-direction: column; justify-content: center; min-height: 205mm; }
      .wordmark { font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 900; font-size: 15pt; letter-spacing: -0.02em; margin: 0 0 auto; }
      .cover h1 { font-size: 40pt; margin-bottom: 14pt; }
      .cover .who { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 17pt; color: var(--ink-2); margin: 0; }

      /* The six types, as a sequence */
      .flow { display: flex; align-items: stretch; gap: 0; margin: 12pt 0 18pt; }
      .flow-step { flex: 1; padding: 9pt 7pt; border-top: 2px solid var(--rule); }
      .flow-step.is-yours { border-top-color: var(--accent); }
      .flow-letter { font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 900; font-size: 16pt; line-height: 1; }
      .flow-name { font-size: 9pt; font-weight: 700; margin-top: 2pt; }
      .flow-what { font-size: 8.5pt; color: var(--ink-3); line-height: 1.35; margin-top: 3pt; }

      /* Ranking */
      .rank { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; }
      .rank td { padding: 7pt 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); vertical-align: baseline; }
      .rank .pos { width: 22pt; color: var(--ink-3); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
      .rank .nm { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 14pt; font-weight: 800; }
      .rank .bar { width: 62pt; }
      .rank .bar span { display: block; height: 6pt; background: var(--accent); border-radius: 3pt; }
      .rank .sc { width: 44pt; text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; color: var(--ink-3); }
      .rank tr.band-start td { border-top: 2px solid var(--ink); }

      .card { border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 4pt; padding: 11pt 12pt; margin: 0 0 10pt; }
      .card h4 { margin: 0 0 4pt; font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 13pt; font-weight: 800; }
      .card .types { font-size: 9pt; color: var(--ink-3); margin: 0 0 6pt; }
      .card p { margin: 0 0 6pt; font-size: 10.5pt; }
      .card p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

      .grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8pt 14pt; }
      .grid .g-name { font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 800; font-size: 11pt; }
      .grid .g-types { font-size: 8.5pt; color: var(--ink-3); }
      .grid .g-line { font-size: 9.5pt; color: var(--ink-2); }

      blockquote { margin: 0 0 10pt; padding-left: 10pt; border-left: 2px solid var(--accent); }
      blockquote .q { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 12pt; }

      footer { margin-top: 18pt; padding-top: 8pt; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); font-size: 8.5pt; color: var(--ink-3); }

      .toolbar { position: sticky; top: 0; display: flex; gap: 10px; justify-content: flex-end; padding: 12px 16px; background: #fff; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
      .toolbar button, .toolbar a { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 13px; font-weight: 700; padding: 9px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid var(--rule); background: #fff; color: var(--ink); cursor: pointer; text-decoration: none; }
      .toolbar button { background: var(--accent); border-color: var(--accent); color: #fff; }
      @media print { .toolbar { display: none; } }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="toolbar">
      <a href="/">Back to Sprint Buddy</a>
      <button type="button" id="print">Save as PDF</button>
    </div>

    {!result ? (
      <div class="sheet">
        <h1>Nothing to print yet</h1>
        <p class="lede">Take the assessment in Sprint Buddy and this becomes your report.</p>
      </div>
    ) : (
      <div class="sheet">

        <!-- 1 Cover -->
        <section class="cover">
          <p class="wordmark">Aalto Founder Sprint</p>
          <h1>Where your energy goes</h1>
          {displayName && <p class="who">{displayName}</p>}
          <p class="who quiet">{result.completedAt}</p>
        </section>

        <!-- 2 How to read this -->
        <section>
          <p class="kicker">How to read this</p>
          <h2>This is about energy, not ability</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            There are six kinds of work in any piece of work. Two of them give you energy, two
            you can do without much cost, and two take energy out of you.
          </p>
          <p>
            The distinction that matters, and the one people get wrong: this is not a measure of
            what you are good at. You can be excellent at something that drains you. Most people
            reading this are, because being good at something is exactly how you end up doing more
            of it. That is the trap this document exists to name. Competence and energy are
            different axes, and a career built only on the first one runs out.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is also worth saying what this is not. It is a self-report, answered on one day,
            about how you behave. It is not a diagnosis, not a fixed trait, and not a reason to
            turn down work. Read it as a description of where your attention goes when nobody is
            making it go anywhere.
          </p>
          <hr class="rule" />
          <p class="quiet">
            The six-type model here is Patrick Lencioni's, from The 6 Types of Working Genius.
            The assessment behind this report is the Sprint's own and is not the official
            instrument.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- 3 The six types -->
        <section>
          <p class="kicker">The six</p>
          <h2>Six kinds of work, in the order work happens</h2>
          <p>
            Every project runs the same route: somebody notices the need, somebody has the idea,
            somebody judges it, somebody gets people moving, somebody supports the work, somebody
            finishes it. Yours are marked.
          </p>
          <div class="flow">
            {WIDGET_ORDER.map((id) => {
              const t = typeById(id);
              const yours = result!.ranking.slice(0, 2).includes(id);
              return (
                <div class={`flow-step${yours ? " is-yours" : ""}`}>
                  <div class="flow-letter">{t.letter}</div>
                  <div class="flow-name">{t.label}</div>
                  <div class="flow-what">{t.activity}</div>
                </div>
              );
            })}
          </div>
          <p class="quiet">
            Nobody has all six. A team that is missing one of them feels it, usually as a stage
            where work reliably stalls.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- 4 At a glance -->
        <section>
          <p class="kicker">At a glance</p>
          <h2>{typeById(result.ranking[0]!).label} and {typeById(result.ranking[1]!).label}</h2>
          <p class="lede">Your two strongest, out of six. Everything else follows from these.</p>
          <table class="rank">
            <tbody>
              {ranked.map((t, i) => (
                <tr class={i === 0 || i === 2 || i === 4 ? "band-start" : ""}>
                  <td class="pos">{i + 1}</td>
                  <td class="nm">{t.label}</td>
                  <td class="bar"><span style={`width:${Math.max(4, Math.round(result!.rates[t.id] * 100))}%`}></span></td>
                  <td class="sc">{result!.counts[t.id]}/{result!.contests[t.id]}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <h3>{bandName("genius")}</h3>
          <p>{result.bands.genius.map((id) => typeById(id).label).join(" and ")}</p>
          <h3>{bandName("competency")}</h3>
          <p>{result.bands.competency.map((id) => typeById(id).label).join(" and ")}</p>
          <h3>{bandName("frustration")}</h3>
          <p>{result.bands.frustration.map((id) => typeById(id).label).join(" and ")}</p>
        </section>

        <!-- 5, 6, 7 the three bands -->
        {(["genius", "competency", "frustration"] as const).map((band) => (
          <section>
            <p class="kicker">{band === "genius" ? "Your strengths" : band === "competency" ? "Your middle" : "Your costs"}</p>
            <h2>{bandName(band)}</h2>
            {result!.bands[band].map((id) => (
              <div>
                <h3>{typeById(id).label}</h3>
                <p class="lede">{bandCopy(typeById(id), band)}</p>
              </div>
            ))}
            {band === "competency" && (
              <>
                <hr class="rule" />
                <p>
                  Worth being careful with this pair. People often become genuinely good at the
                  work in this band, sometimes better than they are at the two above it, because
                  circumstance put them there and they were stubborn enough to learn it. That
                  competence is real. It is still not the same as energy, and a week made mostly
                  of this work will leave you flat without ever being obviously wrong.
                </p>
              </>
            )}
            {band === "frustration" && (
              <>
                <hr class="rule" />
                <p>
                  Two things, and they pull in opposite directions on purpose. Do not build a role
                  out of this pair; sustained time here is what burnout is usually made of. And do
                  not feel bad about it. A frustration is not a weakness or a gap to close, it is
                  a description of where your energy does not come from. Everybody has two.
                </p>
              </>
            )}
          </section>
        ))}

        <!-- 8 Your pairing -->
        <section>
          <p class="kicker">Your pairing</p>
          <h2>{pairing!.name}</h2>
          <p class="quiet">{pairingTypes(pairing!)}</p>
          <p class="lede">{pairing!.inPractice}</p>
          <h3>What it needs</h3>
          <p>{pairing!.thrives}</p>
          <h3>What grinds it down</h3>
          <p>{pairing!.frustrates}</p>
        </section>

        <!-- 9 All fifteen -->
        <section>
          <p class="kicker">The other fourteen</p>
          <h2>So you can recognise your team</h2>
          <p>
            Fifteen combinations, one per pair of types. Worth reading across a founding team:
            the gaps tell you more than the overlaps.
          </p>
          <div class="grid">
            {others.map((p) => (
              <div>
                <div class="g-name">{p.name}</div>
                <div class="g-types">{pairingTypes(p)}</div>
                <div class="g-line">{p.inPractice.split(". ")[0]}.</div>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- 10 Confidence -->
        <section>
          <p class="kicker">Confidence</p>
          <h2>How firm this result is</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            Every pair of types was asked twice, in different words and far apart. That buys a
            reliability check most reports do not have, so here it is rather than a clean number
            that hides its own softness.
          </p>

          <h3>Agreement between the two askings</h3>
          <p>
            You answered the same way both times on {pct(result.consistency)} of the fifteen
            pairs.{" "}
            {result.consistency >= 0.8
              ? "That is high. The ranking below is a real signal rather than a set of coin tosses."
              : result.consistency >= 0.6
              ? "That is moderate. The broad shape is trustworthy; individual positions in the middle are not."
              : "That is low. Read the bands rather than the order inside them, and consider taking it again when you are less rushed."}
          </p>

          {result.contested.length > 0 && (
            <>
              <h3>Ordered by a fallback</h3>
              <p>
                These finished exactly level, on total and on their head-to-head, so the order
                between them came from a rule rather than from your answers:{" "}
                {result.contested.map(([a, b]) => `${typeById(a).label} and ${typeById(b).label}`).join("; ")}.
              </p>
            </>
          )}

          {(result.boundaryMargins.geniusCompetency === 0 || result.boundaryMargins.competencyFrustration === 0) && (
            <>
              <h3>Band lines that were tie-breaks</h3>
              <p>
                {result.boundaryMargins.geniusCompetency === 0 && "The line between your top two and your middle two was a tie-break, not a finding. "}
                {result.boundaryMargins.competencyFrustration === 0 && "The line between your middle two and your bottom two was a tie-break, not a finding. "}
                Treat those two types as sitting together rather than either side of a line.
              </p>
            </>
          )}

          {result.abstentions.length > 0 && (
            <>
              <h3>Questions you answered in your own words</h3>
              <p>
                On {result.abstentions.length} of the thirty you said neither option matched.
                Those were left out of the scoring rather than guessed at, which means the types
                involved were compared fewer times than the others. The ranking accounts for that
                by scoring on wins per comparison rather than on raw totals, but it is one more
                reason to read the shape rather than the exact order.
              </p>
            </>
          )}

          {result.overrides.length > 0 && (
            <>
              <h3>Where what you wrote outranked what you clicked</h3>
              <p>
                On {result.overrides.length}{" "}
                {result.overrides.length === 1 ? "question" : "questions"} your own description
                pointed somewhere other than the option you selected. The description was used.
                It is unprompted and specific, where a click is the nearest of two fits somebody
                else wrote.
              </p>
            </>
          )}
        </section>

        <!-- 11 In your own words -->
        {result.responses && Object.values(result.responses).some((r) => typeof r === "object" && r && (r as { text?: string }).text) && (
          <section>
            <p class="kicker">In your own words</p>
            <h2>What you wrote</h2>
            <p class="lede">
              The forced choice is what produces a ranking. This is what did not fit inside it,
              which is often the more useful half.
            </p>
            {WORKING_GENIUS_ITEMS.map((item) => {
              /* Walked in presentation order rather than over the response
                 object, so the quotes come back in the order they were
                 answered and each one carries the question it answered. An
                 item id on its own would mean nothing to a founder. */
              const raw = result!.responses[item.id];
              if (typeof raw === "string" || !raw) return null;
              const text = (raw as { text?: string }).text;
              if (!text) return null;
              const answer = raw as { choice?: string; resolved?: string };
              const read =
                answer.resolved && answer.resolved !== "neither"
                  ? typeById(answer.resolved as WorkingGeniusId).label
                  : null;
              return (
                <blockquote>
                  <p class="quiet" style="margin-bottom:3pt">{item.prompt}</p>
                  <p class="q">{text}</p>
                  {read && (
                    <p class="quiet" style="margin:4pt 0 0">
                      Read as {read}
                      {answer.choice && answer.choice !== "neither" && answer.choice !== answer.resolved
                        ? `, where you had clicked ${typeById(answer.choice as WorkingGeniusId).label}. What you wrote was used.`
                        : "."}
                    </p>
                  )}
                </blockquote>
              );
            })}
          </section>
        )}

        <!-- 12 What to do with this -->
        <section>
          <p class="kicker">What to do with this</p>
          <h2>Practical, starting this week</h2>

          <h3>For your own work</h3>
          <p>
            Lean into the top two deliberately rather than incidentally. Look at what is on your
            plate this week and find the one thing that sits squarely in them; that is the thing
            to do first and to do properly, not the thing to fit in around the rest.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then find what you volunteer for out of habit that sits in the bottom two, and stop
            volunteering. Not refuse when asked, stop offering. Most of the draining work people
            carry was never assigned to them.
          </p>
          <p>
            When work in your bottom two is genuinely yours to do, put it where your energy is
            highest rather than saving it for the end of the day. Draining work done tired takes
            twice as long and produces the result that makes you dread it more.
          </p>
          <p>
            The signal to learn in the moment is a specific one: not boredom, but a task you keep
            picking up and putting down without progress. That is usually not procrastination. It
            is the shape of the work not matching the shape of your attention.
          </p>

          <h3>With your team</h3>
          <p>
            Swap results with your co-founders. The useful conversation is not who is what, it is
            where the gaps are: read across the six types and find the stage nobody's energy
            covers. That stage is where your work will reliably stall, and knowing which one it is
            beforehand is worth more than any individual result here.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three questions worth asking out loud, in the sprint, with the results on the table.
            Who is doing work that lands in their bottom two, and does it have to be them? Which
            stage of our work has nobody's energy behind it, and what do we do about that,
            deliberately? And what has each of us been quietly carrying because we are good at it
            rather than because it gives us anything back?
          </p>

          <footer>
            Taken {result.completedAt} · instrument {result.version} ·
            Aalto Founder Sprint
          </footer>
        </section>
      </div>
    )}

    <script is:inline>
      document.getElementById("print")?.addEventListener("click", () => window.print());
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
